Definition — GoSafe stage selection (Baumann et al. 2021, Algorithm 1)
Fix a tolerance $\epsilon\gt0$, write $w=u-l$ for the confidence widths and $G$, $M$, $S$ for the current expanders, maximisers and safe set (iteration indices suppressed). At every iteration GoSafe runs

    S1 if $\max\{w(a,x_0,i):\ (a,x_0)\in G\cup M,\ i\in I\}\gt\epsilon$, where only pairs at the nominal initial state $x_0$ are considered;
    otherwise S2 if $\max\{w(a,\tilde x_0,i):\ (a,\tilde x_0)\in G,\ i\in I_g\}\gt\epsilon$;
    otherwise S3, over the pairs $(a,\tilde x_0)\notin E_f\cup S$ with $a\in A$ arbitrary and $\tilde x_0\in P_X(S):=\{\tilde x_0\in X_\mu:\ \exists a'\in A,\ (a',\tilde x_0)\in S\}$, the projection of $S$ onto the state coordinate, i.e. the grid states that already have a certified policy.

  Each stage evaluates a maximiser of its acquisition (table below); ties may be broken arbitrarily (see Primer B). After every iteration, each recorded failure state that no longer lies in the border $\partial S$ is deleted, and its pair leaves $E_f$, so it can be proposed again. There is no separate stopping test: whenever the loop is stopped, GoSafe returns $\hat a=\arg\max_{(a,x_0)\in S}l(a,x_0,0)$.

